INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (1,'sunk_cost','Sunk Cost Fallacy','Continuing because of past investment, not future value.','The sunk cost fallacy occurs when we continue a behavior or endeavor because of previously invested resources — time, money, effort — rather than evaluating the future value of continuing. The rational approach is to only consider future costs and benefits when making decisions, ignoring what has already been spent.\n\nThis is one of the most common cognitive traps in both business and personal life. We feel that abandoning something we have invested in means \"wasting\" those resources, when in reality those resources are gone regardless of our next decision.','A company has spent $5 million developing a product that market research now shows nobody wants. They continue development because \"we have already invested so much\" rather than cutting losses and redirecting resources to a viable product.','Continuing a project because the remaining investment is small and the expected return still justifies it is NOT sunk cost fallacy — that is rational cost-benefit analysis of future value.',1);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (2,'first_principles','First Principles Thinking','Break down to fundamentals, reason up from there.','First principles thinking involves breaking down a problem to its most fundamental truths — the things that cannot be reduced further — and then reasoning up from there. Instead of reasoning by analogy (\"this is how it has always been done\"), you question every assumption.\n\nThis approach is essential when existing solutions are suboptimal or when entering a new domain where analogies from other fields may not apply. It requires more mental effort but often leads to genuinely innovative solutions.','Elon Musk questioning why batteries cost $600/kWh by breaking down the raw material costs (about $80/kWh), then finding ways to assemble them more cheaply rather than accepting the market price as given.','When a well-established best practice exists and your situation closely matches the original context, reasoning from first principles may waste time reinventing the wheel.',2);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (3,'second_order','Second-Order Effects','What happens next after the obvious first consequence.','Second-order thinking means looking beyond the immediate, obvious consequence of a decision to consider what happens next, and what happens after that. Most people stop at first-order effects. The best decision-makers think at least two or three levels deep.\n\nIn business, policy, and personal decisions, second-order effects often dwarf first-order effects. A decision that looks great in the short term may be catastrophic when downstream consequences are considered.','A city imposes rent control (first-order: tenants pay less). Second-order: landlords reduce maintenance and stop building new units. Third-order: housing supply shrinks, quality drops, and prices rise in uncontrolled segments.','Not every decision warrants deep chain-of-consequence analysis. For routine, low-stakes, easily reversible decisions, overthinking downstream effects leads to analysis paralysis.',3);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (4,'inversion','Inversion','Instead of how to succeed, ask how to fail and avoid that.','Inversion means approaching a problem backward. Instead of asking \"How do I achieve X?\", ask \"What would guarantee failure?\" or \"What should I definitely avoid?\" Then make sure you are not doing those things.\n\nThis mental model is powerful because it is often easier to identify what causes failure than what causes success. By systematically eliminating paths to failure, you dramatically increase your odds of a good outcome.','Instead of asking \"How do I build a great company?\", ask \"What kills companies?\" Answers: running out of cash, co-founder conflicts, building something nobody wants, ignoring customers. Then build systems to prevent each one.','Inversion works best for avoiding catastrophic mistakes. It is less useful when you need to generate creative, novel solutions — for that, you need forward-looking ideation, not just failure avoidance.',4);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (5,'base_rate','Base Rate Neglect','Ignoring statistical likelihood in favor of specific details.','Base rate neglect occurs when we ignore the overall probability of an event (the base rate) and focus instead on specific, vivid details of an individual case. Our minds are drawn to narratives and specifics, causing us to overweight them relative to statistical reality.\n\nThis is particularly dangerous in hiring, investing, medical diagnosis, and any domain where individual stories can override data about what usually happens.','A startup founder gives an incredible pitch with a compelling story. Investors get excited and ignore that 90% of startups in this category fail within two years. The base rate of failure should heavily influence the investment decision, but the vivid narrative overrides it.','When you have strong, specific evidence that genuinely differentiates this case from the base rate, it is correct to update your estimate. The key is knowing whether your specific information actually has predictive power.',5);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (6,'confirmation_bias','Confirmation Bias','Seeking info that supports existing beliefs.','Confirmation bias is the tendency to search for, interpret, favor, and recall information in a way that confirms our pre-existing beliefs or hypotheses. We unconsciously filter evidence to support what we already think, and we scrutinize contradictory evidence much more harshly.\n\nThis bias affects every domain — science, business, politics, relationships. The antidote is actively seeking out disconfirming evidence and steelmanning opposing viewpoints.','A manager believes a particular employee is underperforming. From that point on, every mistake the employee makes is noted and remembered, while their successes are attributed to luck or others. The manager only sees evidence that confirms the original assessment.','Having a hypothesis and then testing it with evidence is NOT confirmation bias — that is the scientific method. Confirmation bias specifically means only looking for supporting evidence and ignoring or dismissing contradictory evidence.',6);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (7,'availability','Availability Heuristic','Overweighting recent or vivid examples.','The availability heuristic is a mental shortcut where we estimate the likelihood of events based on how easily examples come to mind. Events that are recent, vivid, emotionally charged, or frequently discussed seem more probable than they actually are.\n\nMedia coverage dramatically amplifies this effect. Plane crashes feel more dangerous than car accidents because they are dramatic and heavily reported, despite cars being statistically far more dangerous.','After seeing news coverage of a shark attack, a vacationer refuses to swim in the ocean despite the statistical risk being about 1 in 11.5 million. Meanwhile, they drive to the beach without concern despite the much higher risk of a car accident.','When your recent experience IS actually representative of the true frequency — for example, if you work in an emergency room and see many car accidents — the availability heuristic may give you an accurate picture rather than a distorted one.',7);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (8,'anchoring','Anchoring','Over-relying on the first piece of information encountered.','Anchoring occurs when we rely too heavily on the first piece of information we receive (the \"anchor\") when making decisions. Subsequent judgments are then made by adjusting away from that anchor, but the adjustment is usually insufficient.\n\nAnchoring affects negotiations, pricing, estimates, and judgments of all kinds. Even when people know an anchor is arbitrary or irrelevant, it still influences their thinking.','A car dealer lists a used car at $25,000. Even if the buyer knows this is inflated, their counteroffer will typically be closer to $25,000 than if the car had been listed at $18,000. The initial price anchored their perception of the car\'s value.','Using a known, reliable reference point to calibrate estimates is smart — that is not anchoring bias. Anchoring becomes a problem when the reference point is arbitrary, irrelevant, or deliberately manipulative.',8);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (9,'opportunity_cost','Opportunity Cost','What you give up by choosing this option.','Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative you forgo when making a choice. Every decision has an opportunity cost — the question is whether you are consciously evaluating it or ignoring it.\n\nPeople tend to evaluate decisions in isolation rather than comparing them to alternatives. The true cost of anything is not just its price tag but also what else you could have done with those resources.','A developer spends three months building a custom CMS instead of using WordPress. The opportunity cost is not just the development time — it is the three months of content, marketing, and revenue they could have generated by shipping sooner with an existing solution.','For very low-cost, easily reversible decisions, calculating opportunity cost is itself a waste of time. The opportunity cost of deliberation can exceed the opportunity cost of the choice itself.',9);
INSERT INTO `tr_mental_models` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `short_desc`, `full_desc`, `example`, `counter_example`, `display_order`) VALUES (10,'occams_razor','Occam\'s Razor','The simplest explanation is usually correct.','Occam\'s Razor states that among competing hypotheses, the one with the fewest assumptions should be selected. It does not say the simplest explanation is always right — it says we should prefer simplicity unless there is evidence requiring a more complex explanation.\n\nThis principle is invaluable for debugging, diagnosing problems, and evaluating theories. When something goes wrong, start with the simplest possible cause before jumping to elaborate theories.','A website goes down. The team starts investigating complex distributed systems failures, potential DDoS attacks, and database corruption. It turns out the SSL certificate expired. The simplest explanation was the correct one.','In genuinely complex systems, the actual cause may be a rare interaction of multiple factors. Occam\'s Razor is a starting point for investigation, not a guarantee. If simple explanations have been ruled out, move to more complex ones.',10);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (1,'The Failing Product Launch','Your SaaS company has spent 18 months and $400,000 developing a new analytics dashboard product. Beta testing with 50 users has shown poor engagement — average session length is under 2 minutes, and only 3 out of 50 beta users say they would pay for it.\n\nYour VP of Engineering argues that with just 3 more months of development and another $80,000, the team can add the features beta users requested. Your head of sales, however, has identified a completely different market opportunity in compliance reporting that multiple prospects are actively requesting and willing to pay for today.\n\nThe board meeting is tomorrow and you need to recommend a direction.',1,'KEY:past investment|already spent|future value|cut losses|regardless of what we spent\nThe $400,000 and 18 months are gone regardless of the next decision. The rational framework here is to compare only the future costs and expected returns of each option. Spending $80,000 more on a product with 6% willingness-to-pay versus redirecting resources to a market with demonstrated demand and active purchase intent is a clear choice when you remove the emotional weight of the sunk investment.','First Principles might seem relevant but the core issue is not about breaking down assumptions — it is about whether past spending should influence the go-forward decision. Second-Order Effects analysis could add nuance but the primary trap here is the emotional pull of the existing investment.','beginner','[\"founder\", \"product_manager\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (2,'The Concert Ticket Dilemma','You bought concert tickets three months ago for $200. On the night of the concert, you are exhausted from a brutal work week, it is raining heavily, and a close friend has invited you to a small dinner party at their home — something you would normally jump at.\n\nYou catch yourself thinking: \"I already paid $200, I have to go to the concert.\" Your partner suggests that you should do whatever would make you happiest tonight, regardless of the ticket cost.\n\nWhat mental model is most relevant to making a good decision here?',1,'KEY:already paid|money is gone|would you go if free|future enjoyment\nThe $200 is spent whether you attend or not — it is a sunk cost. The correct decision framework is: if someone handed you a free concert ticket right now, given how you feel tonight, would you choose the concert or the dinner party? If the answer is the dinner party, go to the dinner party. The $200 should not factor into tonight\'s decision at all.','Opportunity Cost is related but is about comparing alternatives going forward. The key insight here is specifically that the past expenditure should not influence the decision — that is the sunk cost fallacy at work.','beginner',NULL,NULL,1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (3,'The Restructuring Resistance','A manufacturing company reorganized its supply chain two years ago at a cost of $2.3 million. The new structure was supposed to reduce costs by 15% but has actually increased them by 4% due to unforeseen logistics complications.\n\nSeveral executives resist proposals to restructure again, arguing that \"we need to give the current system more time to prove itself\" and \"we cannot just throw away a $2.3 million investment.\" Meanwhile, a competitor using a different supply chain model is consistently undercutting them on price.\n\nThe operations team has modeled a new approach that would cost $600,000 to implement but projects 20% cost savings within the first year based on the competitor\'s proven model.',1,'KEY:throw away investment|already spent|future costs and savings|emotional attachment\nThe $2.3 million is gone. The executives are anchored to the past investment rather than evaluating the future on its own merits. The correct analysis compares only the $600,000 implementation cost against the projected 20% savings. The fact that the current system cost $2.3 million is emotionally painful but economically irrelevant to the go-forward decision.','Anchoring is a reasonable alternative since the executives are anchored to the $2.3M figure, but the primary cognitive error is the unwillingness to abandon a failing approach because of what was already invested — textbook sunk cost.','intermediate','[\"operations\", \"founder\"]','[\"manufacturing\", \"logistics\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (4,'The Hiring Bottleneck','Your startup is struggling to hire engineers. You have been following the standard playbook: posting on job boards, using recruiters (at 20-25% of first-year salary), attending career fairs, and offering competitive Silicon Valley salaries despite being based in Austin.\n\nAfter six months, you have hired only 2 of the 8 engineers you need. Your HR director suggests increasing recruiter fees and raising salary offers by another 15%. A board member suggests acquiring a small company just for its engineering team.\n\nBefore spending more money on the same approaches, you wonder if there is a fundamentally different way to think about this problem.',2,'KEY:break down|fundamental|assumptions|why do we need|what problem are we actually solving\nFirst principles thinking means questioning every assumption. Why do we need 8 engineers? What specific capabilities are we missing? Could we achieve the same output with fewer, more senior people? Could we use contractors for specific modules? Do they need to be in Austin? Are we solving for headcount or for engineering output? By decomposing the actual need rather than accepting \"we need 8 engineers\" as given, entirely different solutions emerge.','Inversion (\"what would guarantee we never hire anyone?\") could provide some insights, but the core opportunity here is questioning the fundamental assumptions about what is actually needed rather than optimizing within the existing framework.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"engineering_manager\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (5,'The Pricing Puzzle','You run a B2B consulting firm. Every competitor charges by the hour, ranging from $150 to $400/hr depending on seniority. Your team has been debating whether to position at $250/hr (mid-market) or $350/hr (premium).\n\nA junior consultant asks a question that stumps the room: \"Why do we charge by the hour at all? Our clients do not actually want hours of our time — they want their problems solved.\"\n\nThis sparks a debate about the fundamental nature of your pricing model.',2,'KEY:fundamental|why do we|assumptions|break down|what does the client actually\nThe junior consultant is applying first principles thinking. The assumption \"consulting is sold by the hour\" is an industry convention, not a law of nature. Breaking it down: clients want outcomes (revenue growth, cost reduction, risk mitigation). Hours are a proxy for value delivered, but a poor one — a brilliant insight in 2 hours may be worth more than 200 hours of mediocre analysis. Value-based pricing, project-based pricing, or performance-based pricing all emerge when you question the fundamental assumption.','Opportunity Cost is loosely relevant (what revenue are you leaving on the table with hourly pricing?) but the scenario is specifically about questioning the foundational assumption of how pricing works in the industry.','beginner','[\"founder\", \"consultant\"]','[\"consulting\", \"professional_services\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (6,'The Education Cost Crisis','A nonprofit organization wants to provide job training to underserved communities. They have been quoted $3,000 per student by traditional vocational training providers for a 12-week program.\n\nWith a $300,000 annual budget, they can serve 100 students per year. Their waiting list has 2,000 people. The board is discussing a fundraising campaign to triple the budget.\n\nThe program director wonders whether the fundamental approach needs rethinking rather than simply raising more money to fund the same model.',2,'KEY:break down|fundamental|what does training actually require|decompose|assumptions about how\nFirst principles: What does effective job training actually require? Breaking it down — it requires knowledge transfer, practice, feedback, and a credential that employers trust. A traditional classroom with an instructor is one way to deliver those, but not the only way. Could peer-taught cohorts, recorded content with live mentoring, employer-partnered apprenticeships, or hybrid models deliver the same outcomes at $500 per student instead of $3,000? That would serve 600 students on the same budget.','Second-Order Effects could analyze consequences of scaling, but the core question is about decomposing the training delivery model to its fundamentals and rebuilding it more efficiently.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"nonprofit_director\"]','[\"education\", \"nonprofit\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (7,'The Remote Work Mandate','A 500-person company is debating whether to mandate a full return to office. The CEO argues it will improve collaboration, mentorship, and culture — all things the company valued pre-pandemic.\n\nThe immediate first-order effects seem clear: more face-to-face interaction, easier impromptu meetings, and visible presence in the office. The HR team is preparing the announcement.\n\nBefore the announcement goes out, the Chief People Officer asks the leadership team to think through what happens after the initial return.',3,'KEY:what happens next|downstream|after the initial|chain of consequences|second effect|then what\nThe first-order effect (more in-person collaboration) is obvious. But second-order effects are significant: top performers with remote options elsewhere will leave (talent drain). The remaining workforce skews toward those with fewer options (adverse selection). Recruiting becomes harder because the talent pool shrinks to commuting distance. Office costs increase while competitive position weakens. Third-order: the best people who left start competing against you or join competitors, amplifying the original talent loss.','Inversion (\"what would guarantee this fails?\") touches on some of these issues but does not capture the chain-of-consequences reasoning that is central here. The scenario is specifically about tracing downstream effects beyond the obvious.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"hr_director\"]','[\"technology\", \"professional_services\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (8,'The Price Cut Decision','An e-commerce company is losing market share to a competitor who prices 20% lower. The sales team is pushing hard for an across-the-board 15% price reduction to remain competitive.\n\nThe CFO runs the numbers: a 15% price cut would require a 40% increase in volume just to maintain current gross profit. But the sales team argues the volume increase is realistic given the price-sensitive market.\n\nWhat framework should the leadership team use to evaluate this decision most thoroughly?',3,'KEY:what happens after|chain|competitor response|downstream|then they will|cascade\nSecond-order thinking is critical here. First order: you cut prices, volume increases. Second order: the competitor responds — they can cut another 10%, triggering a price war neither company can sustain. Third order: margins collapse industry-wide, investment in product quality drops, customer experience suffers. Alternatively, second order: your best customers who were already willing to pay full price now pay less (leaving money on the table), while price-sensitive customers attracted by discounts have higher churn rates and lower lifetime value.','Opportunity Cost is relevant (what else could you do with the margin you are sacrificing?) but misses the critical dynamic of competitive response and cascading market effects that second-order thinking captures.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"sales_director\"]','[\"ecommerce\", \"retail\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (9,'The Free Tier Expansion','A project management SaaS is considering expanding its free tier to include unlimited users (currently capped at 5). The product team argues this will dramatically increase adoption and create a larger funnel for paid conversions.\n\nSignup projections show a potential 300% increase in free accounts. The infrastructure team confirms they can handle the load at a manageable cost increase.',3,'KEY:what happens next|downstream|after the initial adoption|consequences beyond|then what happens\nFirst order: massive adoption increase. Second order: support costs explode because free users still file tickets. Free teams of 20+ have complex needs that stress the product in ways 5-person teams never did. Paid conversion rate drops because teams that previously hit the 5-user cap and upgraded now never need to. Third order: the product roadmap gets pulled toward free-user needs because they are now the majority of the user base, potentially alienating paying customers who fund development.','Base Rate Neglect could apply if the team is ignoring historical data about free-tier conversion rates, but the primary analytical need is tracing the chain of consequences beyond the obvious adoption boost.','advanced','[\"product_manager\", \"founder\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (10,'The Customer Retention Challenge','A subscription box company has a 60% annual churn rate. The marketing team has been focused on acquisition — running campaigns, referral programs, and influencer partnerships. They have spent months brainstorming \"How do we make customers stay longer?\" without making meaningful progress.\n\nThe CEO suggests a different approach to the brainstorming session: instead of asking how to retain customers, ask a different question entirely.',4,'KEY:what causes them to leave|how to guarantee failure|avoid|what makes customers cancel|flip the question\nInversion flips the question: instead of \"How do we retain customers?\", ask \"What guarantees customers cancel?\" Then systematically eliminate those causes. Answers might include: product quality declining, boxes feeling generic and impersonal, no community or engagement between shipments, surprise charges, difficult cancellation process that breeds resentment. Each of these is actionable and specific — far more useful than vague retention brainstorming.','First Principles could help decompose what customers actually value, but the specific power here is the inverted approach — identifying and eliminating causes of failure rather than trying to generate causes of success.','beginner','[\"product_manager\", \"founder\"]','[\"ecommerce\", \"subscription\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (11,'The New Manager Playbook','A talented individual contributor has just been promoted to manage a team of 8 engineers. She is eager to succeed and asks her mentor for advice on how to be a great manager.\n\nHer mentor, a VP with 15 years of leadership experience, gives her surprising advice: \"Don\'t start by asking how to be a great manager. Start by asking a completely different question.\"\n\nWhat mental model is the mentor suggesting?',4,'KEY:what would make me a terrible manager|how to fail|avoid|what should I never do|invert\nThe mentor is suggesting inversion. Instead of \"How do I become a great manager?\", ask \"What would make me a terrible manager?\" The answers are clearer and more actionable: micromanaging, taking credit for the team\'s work, avoiding difficult conversations, playing favorites, not shielding the team from organizational chaos, failing to give feedback, and being unavailable. Avoiding these failure modes puts you ahead of most managers without needing to figure out what \"great\" looks like.','Confirmation Bias might be relevant if she only seeks feedback that confirms she is doing well, but the mentor is specifically suggesting the inverted approach to problem-solving.','beginner',NULL,NULL,1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (12,'The Product Launch Risk Assessment','A fintech startup is preparing to launch a new payment processing feature. The team has been focused on the launch plan: marketing, press release, feature documentation, onboarding flow, and customer success preparation.\n\nThe CTO raises a concern in the final planning meeting: \"We have spent all our time planning for success. I think we need to spend a few hours planning for failure.\" She wants the team to think about this differently.\n\nWhat framework is the CTO proposing?',4,'KEY:plan for failure|what could go wrong|pre-mortem|avoid|what would cause this to fail\nThe CTO is proposing inversion — specifically a pre-mortem exercise. Instead of \"How do we make this launch succeed?\", ask \"It is six months from now and the launch was a disaster. What went wrong?\" This inverted framing surfaces risks the team has been unconsciously avoiding: payment processing errors at scale, regulatory compliance gaps, security vulnerabilities, customer confusion, support team overwhelm, and integration failures with existing systems.','Second-Order Effects analysis could help think through consequences of the launch, but the CTO is specifically suggesting an inverted approach — imagining failure first and working backward to prevent it.','intermediate','[\"product_manager\", \"engineering_manager\"]','[\"fintech\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (13,'The Star Candidate','Your company is hiring a VP of Sales. One candidate blows everyone away in interviews — charismatic, articulate, with a compelling narrative about how she grew revenue 5x at her last startup. The interview panel unanimously wants to extend an offer immediately.\n\nBefore the offer goes out, you check the data: industry-wide, externally hired VPs of Sales at companies your size have a 55% failure rate within 18 months. The candidate\'s previous company also had strong market tailwinds that contributed significantly to the growth.\n\nYour head of HR asks the team to reconsider the decision in light of this data.',5,'KEY:base rate|statistical|overall probability|how often|ignore the data|specific story overrides\nThe interview panel is falling prey to base rate neglect. The vivid, compelling interview narrative is overriding the statistical reality that the majority of external VP of Sales hires fail at this stage. The 5x growth story is specific and memorable, but the 55% failure rate is the backdrop against which this hire should be evaluated. This does not mean you should not hire her — it means the process should include additional diligence, structured references, trial periods, or work samples to determine if she genuinely beats the base rate.','Confirmation Bias could be at play if interviewers only asked questions that confirmed their positive impression, but the core issue is the specific narrative overriding statistical likelihood.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"hr_director\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (14,'The Startup Investment','An angel investor is evaluating a pitch from a founder building an AI-powered legal research tool. The demo is impressive, the founder has a law degree from Harvard and an ML PhD from MIT, and two major law firms have signed letters of intent.\n\nThe investor\'s partner points out that in their portfolio of 40 investments over the past decade, companies in the legal tech space have had a 5% success rate (defined as returning more than 1x invested capital). Across all their investments, the success rate is 22%.\n\nThe investor dismisses this, saying \"this one is different.\"',5,'KEY:base rate|statistical likelihood|overall probability|90% of|historical data|this one is different\nThe investor is exhibiting classic base rate neglect. The vivid specifics — Harvard, MIT, impressive demo, signed LOIs — feel like strong signals. But the base rate tells us that 95% of legal tech investments in their own portfolio failed. \"This one is different\" is what every investor says about every investment that later fails. The correct approach is to start with the 5% base rate and ask whether the specific evidence is strong enough to substantially update that probability, not to ignore the base rate entirely.','Availability Heuristic could apply if the investor is recalling their one successful legal tech investment vividly, but the primary error is ignoring the statistical base rate in favor of the specific narrative.','beginner','[\"founder\", \"investor\"]','[\"venture_capital\", \"legal_tech\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (15,'The Marketing Channel Bet','A D2C brand is deciding where to invest its $500,000 marketing budget. The marketing director is excited about TikTok after seeing a competitor\'s viral video generate $2 million in sales overnight. She wants to allocate 60% of the budget to TikTok content creation and influencer partnerships.\n\nThe data analyst presents research showing that across 200 D2C brands in their category, the median ROI on TikTok marketing spend is 0.8x (a loss), with the top 10% achieving 3x+ returns. Meanwhile, Google search ads show a median ROI of 2.1x with less variance.\n\nThe marketing director argues the viral competitor proves TikTok can work.',5,'KEY:base rate|median|statistical|one example|overall data|most companies\nThe marketing director is neglecting the base rate. One viral success story (vivid, specific, recent) is overriding the statistical reality that the median D2C brand loses money on TikTok marketing. The competitor\'s success is an outlier in the top 10%, not a representative outcome. The base rate suggests Google ads are a much safer bet for most of the budget, with perhaps a small experimental allocation to TikTok to test whether this brand can beat the median.','Availability Heuristic is closely related — the vivid viral video is easily recalled — but the core analytical error is specifically about ignoring statistical base rates in favor of a single compelling example.','intermediate','[\"marketing_director\", \"founder\"]','[\"ecommerce\", \"consumer\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (16,'The Product Direction Debate','A product team has been building a mobile-first strategy for two years. The PM believes mobile is the future and has been collecting data to support this view: mobile traffic is up 40%, mobile app ratings are 4.8 stars, and mobile conversion rates have improved quarter over quarter.\n\nA data analyst digs deeper and finds that desktop still generates 73% of revenue, desktop users have 3x the lifetime value, and the mobile conversion improvement is largely due to a segment of low-value impulse purchases. The PM dismisses these findings as \"lagging indicators\" and insists mobile metrics are the leading ones that matter.\n\nWhat cognitive trap is the PM falling into?',6,'KEY:only looking at supporting|ignoring contradictory|dismissing evidence|seeking information that confirms|filtering\nThe PM is exhibiting confirmation bias. She has a pre-existing belief (mobile-first is correct) and is selectively highlighting data that supports it while dismissing contradictory evidence. The mobile metrics she cites are real but cherry-picked. The desktop revenue data directly challenges her thesis, and rather than integrating it, she reclassifies it as irrelevant. A more honest analysis would weigh all the evidence and potentially conclude that a dual-platform strategy is warranted.','Availability Heuristic could apply if the PM is overweighting recent mobile trends, but the specific behavior — actively seeking supporting evidence and dismissing contradictory evidence — is the hallmark of confirmation bias.','intermediate','[\"product_manager\", \"data_analyst\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (17,'The Hiring Manager\'s Gut','A hiring manager interviews a candidate and within the first 5 minutes forms a strong positive impression. For the remaining 55 minutes, the manager asks softball questions, interprets ambiguous answers favorably, and mentally explains away a gap in the candidate\'s resume.\n\nAfter the interview, the manager gives a strong hire recommendation. When a colleague points out that the candidate\'s technical assessment score was below the hiring bar, the manager argues that \"the assessment does not capture their real ability\" and \"they were probably just nervous.\"\n\nWhat is happening here?',6,'KEY:formed opinion first|seeking confirming|explaining away|only favorable|dismissing contradictory evidence\nThis is textbook confirmation bias. The manager formed a conclusion in the first 5 minutes and then spent the rest of the interview unconsciously collecting evidence to support that conclusion. Softball questions are designed (unconsciously) to elicit positive responses. Ambiguous answers are interpreted favorably. Contradictory evidence (the below-bar assessment) is rationalized away rather than integrated into the evaluation. A structured interview process with predetermined questions helps counteract this.','Anchoring is related — the first impression serves as an anchor — but the ongoing behavior of seeking confirming evidence and dismissing contradictory evidence throughout the entire process is specifically confirmation bias.','beginner','[\"engineering_manager\", \"hr_director\"]',NULL,1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (18,'The Strategy Offsite','A CEO has been publicly committed to an international expansion strategy for the past year. At the annual strategy offsite, she presents market research her team compiled showing strong demand signals in three target countries.\n\nThe CFO presents a contrasting analysis: two competitors who expanded to those same markets pulled out within 18 months, the regulatory costs are 3x initial estimates, and customer acquisition costs abroad are 5x domestic. The head of sales adds that their biggest domestic competitor is vulnerable and a domestic push could capture significant market share.\n\nThe CEO thanks them for the input but steers the conversation back to \"how we execute the international plan\" rather than \"whether we should.\"\n\nWhat mental model best describes the CEO\'s behavior?',6,'KEY:committed to a position|dismissing contradictory|steering away from disconfirming|only supporting evidence\nThe CEO has publicly committed to a position and is now filtering all information through that lens. She commissioned research that supports expansion (confirmation-seeking), dismisses contradictory evidence from the CFO and sales lead, and reframes the discussion from \"should we?\" to \"how do we?\" — effectively removing the option of changing course. This is confirmation bias compounded by public commitment, which makes it even harder to objectively evaluate disconfirming evidence.','Sunk Cost Fallacy could apply if the company had already invested heavily in international infrastructure, but the scenario describes a planning-stage commitment. The primary issue is selectively processing information to support a pre-existing belief.','advanced','[\"founder\", \"consultant\"]','[\"consulting\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (19,'The Security Overreaction','After a high-profile data breach at a competitor makes national news, a company\'s board demands an emergency $2 million investment in cybersecurity — specifically in preventing the exact type of breach that was reported (SQL injection attack).\n\nThe CISO points out that their annual security audit, completed just last month, identified insider threats and phishing as their top two vulnerabilities, with SQL injection ranked relatively low due to existing protections. The board insists: \"Did you see what happened to them? We cannot let that happen to us.\"\n\nWhat is driving the board\'s decision-making?',7,'KEY:recent news|vivid example|easily recalled|overweighting|dramatic event|because they saw it\nThe board is being driven by the availability heuristic. The competitor\'s breach is recent, vivid, emotionally charged, and heavily reported — making it feel like the most likely threat. Their own security audit, based on systematic analysis of their specific vulnerabilities, should be more informative than one dramatic external event. The $2 million would be better spent on the insider threat and phishing vulnerabilities that their actual risk assessment identified as higher priority.','Base Rate Neglect is related — the board is ignoring the base rates of different attack types — but the primary driver is the vividness and recency of the competitor\'s breach making that specific threat feel disproportionately likely.','intermediate','[\"founder\", \"engineering_manager\"]','[\"technology\", \"finance\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (20,'The Hiring Freeze Fear','A startup founder reads three articles in one week about tech layoffs at major companies. Two friends at different companies also mention their teams are being downsized. The founder becomes convinced that a major recession is imminent and implements a hiring freeze, despite her company growing 30% quarter-over-quarter with strong unit economics.\n\nHer CFO argues that their metrics do not support a freeze and that their specific market segment (healthcare SaaS) is actually counter-cyclical. The founder responds: \"Look at what is happening everywhere. We need to be cautious.\"',7,'KEY:recent articles|friends mentioned|vivid stories|feels like everywhere|overweighting what she sees and hears\nThe founder is succumbing to the availability heuristic. Three articles and two anecdotes have created a feeling that layoffs are \"everywhere,\" overriding her own company\'s strong data. Media coverage of layoffs is dramatic and frequent, making the threat feel more pervasive than it may be. Her own company\'s 30% growth and strong unit economics in a counter-cyclical segment are better predictors of her company\'s future than anecdotes from unrelated companies in different market conditions.','Confirmation Bias could apply if she was already anxious and seeking out negative news, but the scenario specifically describes recent vivid information creating a distorted sense of probability — classic availability heuristic.','beginner','[\"founder\"]','[\"saas\", \"healthcare\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (21,'The Feature Priority Misjudgment','A product manager receives five angry support tickets in one day from enterprise customers about a reporting export bug. She immediately escalates and pushes to reprioritize the sprint to fix it, calling it \"the most critical issue facing our customers.\"\n\nThe support lead pulls up the data: in the past month, there have been 7 tickets about the export bug (out of 2,000 total tickets), compared to 340 tickets about slow dashboard loading and 180 about confusing onboarding. The export bug just happened to cluster on one day.',7,'KEY:five in one day|vivid|clustered|felt like|recent experience|overweighting|seemed more common\nThe PM is experiencing the availability heuristic. Five tickets arriving on the same day created a vivid, recent cluster that made the issue feel far more prevalent and critical than it actually is. In the broader data, it represents 0.35% of support volume — a minor issue. The dashboard performance problem affects 50x more users but does not feel as urgent because those tickets arrive steadily rather than in a dramatic cluster.','Anchoring could apply if the PM was fixated on the number 5, but the primary issue is the recency and vividness of a clustered experience distorting her perception of the problem\'s prevalence.','beginner','[\"product_manager\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (22,'The Salary Negotiation','A candidate for a senior engineering role currently earns $145,000. During the interview process, the recruiter asks for their current salary. The hiring manager now has this number in mind.\n\nThe market rate for this role at this company is $180,000-$200,000. The candidate is exceptionally qualified and received a strong hire recommendation from every interviewer. The hiring manager proposes offering $165,000 — a \"significant increase\" over the candidate\'s current pay.\n\nThe HR business partner flags that $165,000 is below the bottom of the approved range for this role.',8,'KEY:first number|current salary|reference point|influenced by|starting point|adjusted from\nThe hiring manager is anchored to the candidate\'s current salary of $145,000. Despite the role being worth $180-200K and the candidate being exceptionally qualified, the $145K figure pulled the offer downward. The \"significant increase\" framing reveals the anchoring — the manager is adjusting up from $145K rather than evaluating the role\'s market value independently. Without knowing the current salary, the offer would likely have been in the approved $180-200K range.','Opportunity Cost could apply (the cost of losing this candidate to a competitor who pays market rate), but the specific cognitive mechanism is anchoring — the first number encountered is disproportionately influencing the decision.','beginner','[\"hr_director\", \"engineering_manager\"]',NULL,1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (23,'The Project Estimate Trap','A development team is estimating a new feature. The tech lead casually mentions in the planning meeting: \"The last similar feature took about 3 weeks.\" This was meant as context, not an estimate.\n\nThe team then discusses the new feature in detail. It is significantly more complex — involving a new API integration, a database migration, and a redesigned UI. Despite identifying all these additional complexities, the team converges on an estimate of 4.5 weeks.\n\nWhen an outside consultant later reviews the same scope without hearing the \"3 weeks\" comment, she estimates 8-10 weeks.',8,'KEY:3 weeks|first number mentioned|adjusted insufficiently|starting point|influenced by the initial\nThe team was anchored by the tech lead\'s casual mention of \"3 weeks.\" Even though they identified significant additional complexity, their adjustment from the anchor was insufficient — moving from 3 to 4.5 weeks instead of the more realistic 8-10 weeks. This is classic anchoring: the first number creates a gravitational pull, and subsequent adjustments are typically too small. The outside consultant, without exposure to the anchor, arrived at a dramatically different (and likely more accurate) estimate.','First Principles thinking could help decompose the estimate from fundamentals, but the scenario specifically illustrates how an arbitrary initial number distorts group estimation.','intermediate','[\"engineering_manager\", \"product_manager\"]','[\"technology\", \"saas\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (24,'The Acquisition Offer','A bootstrapped SaaS founder receives an unsolicited acquisition offer for $5 million. She turns it down, believing her company is worth more. Six months later, growth has stalled and she decides to explore a sale.\n\nHer investment banker values the company at $3-4 million based on current metrics. The founder insists on listing at $5.5 million, refusing to accept anything below $5 million.\n\nWhen asked why $5M is the floor, she says: \"Someone was willing to pay $5 million six months ago when we were growing faster. We cannot take less than that now.\"\n\nWhat is driving her pricing floor?',8,'KEY:first offer|$5 million|reference point|original number|adjusting from|past offer anchoring\nThe founder is anchored to the original $5 million offer. That number has become her psychological reference point, and she is unwilling to adjust downward sufficiently despite changed circumstances (stalled growth, lower valuation by her own banker). The rational approach would be to ignore the historical offer entirely and value the company based on current metrics. The $5M figure is anchoring her to a number that no longer reflects reality.','Sunk Cost Fallacy has elements here (she invested time and effort building the company), but the specific issue is the $5M number serving as an immovable anchor rather than an emotional attachment to past investment.','intermediate','[\"founder\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (25,'The Build vs. Buy Decision','A startup CTO wants to build a custom authentication system. Her rationale: \"It will be exactly what we need, we will own the code, and it avoids vendor lock-in.\" She estimates 6 weeks of engineering time for two senior developers.\n\nThe alternative is using Auth0 or a similar service at $500/month. The CTO argues that the custom build will save money in the long run compared to ongoing SaaS fees.\n\nThe CEO asks her to think about what those two senior engineers would be working on during those 6 weeks if they were not building auth.',9,'KEY:what else could they build|alternative use|give up|those 6 weeks|instead of|trade-off\nThe CEO is highlighting opportunity cost. The explicit cost of building auth is 6 weeks of engineering time. But the opportunity cost is whatever those two senior engineers would have built instead — features that could drive revenue, reduce churn, or create competitive advantage. At $200K/year per engineer, 6 weeks costs roughly $46K in salary alone, plus $500/month is $6K/year for Auth0. But the real cost is not the salary — it is the 6 weeks of product development that did not happen during a critical growth phase.','First Principles could help analyze whether custom auth is truly needed, but the specific insight the CEO is driving at is the opportunity cost — not whether to build auth, but what you sacrifice by choosing to build it.','beginner','[\"founder\", \"engineering_manager\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (26,'The Conference Circuit','A founder spends 8-10 days per month attending conferences, networking events, and speaking engagements. She considers this essential for building brand awareness and generating leads.\n\nHer co-founder runs the numbers: in the past quarter, conference-sourced leads generated $40,000 in pipeline. During that same quarter, the founder was absent for 30+ days — days when she could have been closing existing pipeline ($200,000 in stalled deals), building the enterprise sales process, or developing the two key features that prospects keep requesting.\n\nThe co-founder is not saying conferences are bad. He is asking a more specific question.',9,'KEY:what else could she do|those 30 days|alternative use of time|give up|instead of|trade-off\nThe co-founder is raising opportunity cost. The conferences generate some value ($40K in pipeline), but the relevant question is not \"are conferences valuable?\" — it is \"are conferences more valuable than the alternatives?\" The $200K in stalled deals, the sales process build-out, and the requested features all compete for the same scarce resource: the founder\'s time. The opportunity cost of 30 conference days is the value of whatever the next-best use of those 30 days would have been.','Second-Order Effects could analyze downstream consequences of each choice, but the core framework here is the direct comparison of mutually exclusive uses of a scarce resource — textbook opportunity cost.','beginner','[\"founder\", \"sales_director\"]','[\"saas\", \"consulting\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (27,'The Perfectionism Trap','A design team has been polishing a landing page redesign for three weeks. The current version tested well — 85% of user testers preferred it over the existing page. The lead designer wants two more weeks to reach what she calls \"pixel perfection.\"\n\nThe marketing director points out that the current page has a 2.1% conversion rate. Even a modest improvement from the tested redesign could generate significant additional revenue. Every day the new page is not live is a day of potential conversions lost.\n\nThe marketing director is making an argument based on what specific framework?',9,'KEY:every day not live|revenue lost|what you give up|time spent polishing|could be converting|trade-off\nThe marketing director is arguing opportunity cost. The two weeks of additional polishing have an explicit cost (designer time) but more importantly, an opportunity cost: 14 days of improved conversions that will never happen. If the redesign improves conversion from 2.1% to even 2.5% and the page gets 10,000 visitors per day, those two weeks represent hundreds of lost conversions. The cost of \"pixel perfection\" is measured in what you give up while pursuing it.','First Principles could question whether pixel perfection actually improves conversions, but the marketing director\'s specific argument is about the opportunity cost of delay — what is lost while waiting.','beginner','[\"product_manager\", \"marketing_director\"]','[\"saas\", \"ecommerce\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (28,'The Conversion Drop Mystery','An e-commerce site\'s conversion rate dropped 40% overnight. The team launches into emergency mode. The head of growth suspects a competitor launched a major promotion. The CTO wonders if their recommendation algorithm has degraded. The VP of Marketing thinks their recent brand campaign may have attracted low-intent traffic.\n\nEach team begins investigating their theory. Meanwhile, a junior developer checks the checkout page and discovers that yesterday\'s deploy broke the \"Place Order\" button on mobile — it is hidden below the fold and unreachable on most phone screens.\n\nWhat principle did the junior developer apply that the senior leaders missed?',10,'KEY:simplest explanation|most straightforward|fewest assumptions|obvious cause first|simple before complex\nThe junior developer applied Occam\'s Razor — start with the simplest possible explanation before building complex theories. A broken checkout button explains a 40% conversion drop with a single assumption (the deploy broke something). The other theories each require multiple assumptions about competitor behavior, algorithm degradation, or traffic quality changes. The simplest explanation — something is literally broken — should always be checked first.','First Principles could help decompose the conversion funnel, but the specific lesson is about preferring the simplest explanation and checking obvious causes before constructing elaborate theories.','beginner','[\"engineering_manager\", \"product_manager\"]','[\"ecommerce\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (29,'The Employee Performance Puzzle','A previously high-performing employee\'s work quality has noticeably declined over the past month. Her manager considers several explanations: she is disengaged and looking for a new job, she is being poached by a competitor, the recent team restructuring has demotivated her, or she disagrees with the product direction and is quietly protesting.\n\nBefore acting on any of these theories, a colleague suggests the manager simply talk to the employee first. It turns out she has been dealing with a family medical emergency and has been too proud to ask for support or accommodations.\n\nWhat principle was the colleague applying?',10,'KEY:simplest explanation|ask directly|fewest assumptions|straightforward|simple before complex\nThe colleague applied Occam\'s Razor. The simplest explanation for a sudden performance change is a personal circumstance — it requires only one assumption (something changed in her life) rather than complex theories about engagement, competitors, or silent protests. Each of those alternative explanations requires multiple assumptions about the employee\'s inner motivations and external influences. The simplest diagnostic step — asking directly — was also the most efficient.','Inversion (\"what would cause a good employee to suddenly underperform?\") could generate the same answer, but the guiding principle is specifically about preferring the simplest explanation and testing it first.','beginner',NULL,NULL,1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (30,'The System Slowdown Investigation','A financial services platform has been experiencing intermittent slowdowns for two weeks. The infrastructure team has proposed three theories: (1) a subtle memory leak in the new microservice deployed three weeks ago, (2) network congestion from a neighboring tenant in their cloud environment, or (3) database query optimization degradation due to data growth patterns.\n\nEach theory requires significant investigation — instrumenting the microservice, engaging the cloud provider, or analyzing query plans across hundreds of endpoints.\n\nA senior SRE asks one question: \"Did anything change three weeks ago besides the microservice deploy?\" The team checks and discovers that a cron job was added that runs every 15 minutes and does a full table scan on the largest database table.',10,'KEY:simplest explanation|what changed|fewest assumptions|straightforward cause|simple before complex\nThe SRE applied Occam\'s Razor. The simplest explanation for a system that worked fine and then started having problems is that something changed. A new cron job doing full table scans every 15 minutes is a single, clear cause that explains intermittent slowdowns with no additional assumptions. The other theories each require multiple assumptions about leak patterns, cloud infrastructure behavior, or gradual data changes. Start with \"what changed?\" — it is almost always the answer.','First Principles could help decompose system performance from fundamentals, but the specific principle is about starting with the simplest possible explanation before investing in complex investigations.','intermediate','[\"engineering_manager\"]','[\"fintech\", \"technology\"]',1,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (31,'The Pivot Paralysis','A startup has spent 14 months and $1.2 million building an AI-powered recruiting platform. The product works technically, but adoption is painfully slow — only 12 paying customers after 8 months of sales effort. The CEO keeps saying \"we just need more time and better marketing.\"\n\nMeanwhile, three of their 12 customers independently asked if the company could adapt their NLP technology for a completely different use case: analyzing customer support tickets to predict churn. One customer offered to pay $50K for a pilot.\n\nThe CTO wants to pivot. The CEO insists they would be \"throwing away\" their recruiting product. The board is split. A senior advisor suggests they think about this from multiple angles before deciding.',1,'KEY:sunk cost|past investment|opportunity cost|what else could we build|future value vs past spending\nThis scenario involves at least two major mental models. The CEO is trapped by sunk cost fallacy — the $1.2M and 14 months are gone regardless of the next decision. But the advisor is also pointing toward opportunity cost: every month spent on the struggling recruiting product is a month not spent on the churn-prediction opportunity that already has a $50K pilot offer. The interplay matters because sunk cost explains WHY the CEO is stuck, while opportunity cost clarifies WHAT the right comparison framework is for the go-forward decision.','First Principles could help decompose what the technology actually does vs. what market it serves, but the primary tension is between the emotional pull of past investment (sunk cost) and the rational comparison of future alternatives (opportunity cost).','advanced','[\"founder\", \"product_manager\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,1,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (32,'The Scaling Trap','A D2C skincare brand went viral on TikTok last month. Sales jumped from $80K/month to $400K/month overnight. The founder is ecstatic and wants to double down: she has signed a $200K influencer deal, committed to 3x inventory from their manufacturer, and is hiring 8 customer service reps.\n\nHer CFO raises concerns: the viral product is a single SKU (a $24 serum), the TikTok algorithm is unpredictable, and their customer acquisition cost during the viral period was $2 — but historically it is $18. If the viral wave fades, they will be sitting on $600K of inventory with fixed payroll commitments.\n\nThe founder dismisses the CFO\'s concerns as \"not understanding how social media works\" and points to her overflowing inbox of press inquiries as proof the momentum is real.',7,'KEY:viral|recent success|vivid|second-order|what happens after|downstream consequences\nTwo models are at work here. The founder is experiencing availability heuristic — the viral success is recent, vivid, and emotionally charged, making her overestimate the likelihood it will continue. Press inquiries and a full inbox reinforce the feeling that \"this is the new normal.\" But the CFO is applying second-order thinking: first order (sales spike) is obvious; second order (what happens when the algorithm shifts, inventory sits, and fixed costs remain) is where the real risk lives. The interplay is critical: availability heuristic explains WHY the founder is overconfident, and second-order thinking is the FRAMEWORK the CFO is correctly applying to evaluate the downstream risks.','Anchoring to the $2 CAC is related but secondary — the core dynamic is availability-driven overconfidence colliding with the need for second-order consequence analysis.','advanced','[\"founder\", \"marketing_director\"]','[\"ecommerce\", \"consumer\"]',1,1,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (33,'The Consensus Hire','A startup\'s leadership team unanimously wants to hire a particular VP of Product candidate. She interviewed brilliantly, told compelling stories about her past successes, and every interviewer gave a strong hire recommendation within 24 hours.\n\nThe head of people pauses and asks the team to consider two things: first, the overall base rate of success for externally hired VPs at their stage is about 40%. Second, she noticed that every interviewer asked different questions, interpreted the same stories differently, and yet all arrived at the same positive conclusion — almost as if they decided early and spent the rest of the interview confirming.\n\nShe is not saying \"don\'t hire her.\" She is saying \"slow down and add more rigor to the process.\"',6,'KEY:confirmation bias|formed opinion early|base rate|statistical likelihood|both biases\nTwo distinct cognitive traps are at play. Confirmation bias: each interviewer formed a positive impression early and unconsciously spent the remaining time confirming it. The fact that they asked different questions but reached the same conclusion is actually evidence of bias, not evidence of quality — a structured process with consistent questions would be more reliable. Base rate neglect: the team is ignoring the 40% success rate for this type of hire, allowing the vivid interview narrative to override statistical reality. The head of people is correctly identifying both: the process was contaminated by confirmation bias, AND the base rate should make them more cautious regardless of interview impressions.','Availability heuristic is adjacent (the compelling stories are vivid) but the specific mechanisms are confirmation-seeking behavior during the interview process and ignoring the statistical base rate of VP hiring success.','advanced','[\"hr_director\", \"founder\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,1,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (34,'The Market Entry Mirage','A European SaaS company is considering entering the US market. They have built a detailed 50-page market analysis showing strong demand, favorable unit economics, and three US competitors with known weaknesses. The CEO presents this analysis to the board with confidence.\n\nA board member notes that the analysis was commissioned specifically to evaluate US entry — the conclusion the CEO already wanted to reach. She asks: \"Did we also commission an analysis of what would happen if we invested the same $3 million in deepening our European market share instead?\" The room goes quiet.\n\nThe CEO recovers and says the US market is 10x larger, making it the obvious choice. Another board member points out that of the last five European SaaS companies in their category that entered the US, four retreated within two years.',6,'KEY:confirmation bias|sought supporting evidence|opportunity cost|alternative use|base rate\nThree models intersect here. Confirmation bias is primary: the CEO commissioned research specifically to support a predetermined conclusion. The analysis was designed to confirm, not to genuinely evaluate. Opportunity cost is the board member\'s counterpoint: the $3M could deepen European market share instead, and that alternative was never evaluated. Base rate neglect layers on top: the 80% failure rate of similar European-to-US expansions should heavily inform the decision but is being overridden by the detailed (but biased) analysis. The interplay: confirmation bias contaminated the information gathering, which prevented honest opportunity cost comparison, while base rate neglect is allowing the team to dismiss historical evidence.','First Principles could help decompose what US entry actually requires, but the scenario specifically illustrates the compounding effect of multiple cognitive biases reinforcing each other.','advanced','[\"founder\", \"consultant\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,1,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (35,'The Feature Factory','A product team has been shipping features at an impressive pace — 3 major releases per quarter for the past year. The VP of Product proudly presents the roadmap at all-hands meetings, highlighting velocity metrics. Engineering morale is high because they are shipping.\n\nBut the customer success team has a different story. Churn is creeping up. Support tickets mention \"too complex\" and \"can\'t find what I need.\" A recent NPS survey dropped from 52 to 34. When confronted with this data, the VP of Product argues that \"shipping more features is what customers asked for in our survey\" and that \"NPS is a lagging indicator.\"\n\nA product advisor suggests the team is confusing motion with progress, and asks them to think about what they are NOT doing while they are busy shipping.',9,'KEY:opportunity cost|what are we not doing|confirmation bias|only hearing what supports|time spent shipping\nOpportunity cost is the central framework: every sprint spent building new features is a sprint NOT spent on improving existing features, fixing UX debt, or addressing the complexity that is driving churn. The advisor\'s question — \"what are you NOT doing?\" — is pure opportunity cost thinking. But confirmation bias is also at work: the VP selectively cites survey data that supports the shipping-more strategy while dismissing NPS as a \"lagging indicator.\" The survey likely contained mixed signals, but the VP filtered for the data that confirmed the existing approach. The interplay: confirmation bias prevents the team from recognizing the opportunity cost of their current strategy.','Second-order effects (shipping creates complexity, complexity causes churn) is related but the primary analytical need is recognizing the opportunity cost of feature velocity and the confirmation bias in how data is being interpreted.','advanced','[\"product_manager\", \"founder\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,1,NULL);
INSERT INTO `tr_scenarios` (`id`, `title`, `situation`, `correct_model_id`, `ideal_reasoning`, `distractor_explanation`, `difficulty`, `role_tags`, `industry_tags`, `is_active`, `is_mashup`, `pack_id`) VALUES (36,'The Acquisition Distraction','A mid-stage startup receives an acquisition offer for $30 million. The founders have invested 5 years building the company. The offer is 3x their last valuation round, which happened 18 months ago when growth was faster.\n\nThe CEO leans toward rejecting the offer because \"we have already put 5 years into this and we are just getting started.\" The CTO wants to accept because \"the market is shifting and our window may be closing.\" A board member complicates things by saying: \"The $30M offer is anchoring your thinking. What would you do if no offer existed?\"\n\nMeanwhile, a competitor just raised $50 million and is hiring aggressively in the founders\' core market.',1,'KEY:sunk cost|5 years invested|anchoring|$30M number|inversion|what if no offer\nThree models collide here. The CEO is exhibiting sunk cost fallacy: \"we have already put 5 years into this\" is textbook — those years are gone regardless. Anchoring is the board member\'s insight: the $30M figure is now the reference point for all subsequent thinking about the company\'s value, even though the number is arbitrary (it is what someone offered, not what the company is worth). The board member\'s question — \"what would you do if no offer existed?\" — is actually applying inversion: remove the anchor entirely and evaluate the business on its own merits. The interplay: sunk cost makes the CEO want to keep going for emotional reasons, while anchoring distorts both the CEO and CTO\'s ability to evaluate the business objectively.','Opportunity cost (what else could the founders do with $30M?) and second-order effects (what happens if the competitor dominates?) are relevant but secondary to the three primary biases at work.','advanced','[\"founder\", \"investor\"]','[\"saas\", \"technology\"]',1,1,NULL);
